From a52d5633c1e9eb3f3b47a3d51340090bc40913b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:20:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] scratch archive: leave out git objects the remote
 already holds (core clone archive 57-80 % -> 0.7 %)

Why: on worker-3 (t_01a1d52568eb39ad, 13:34Z pass) 12 reclaimed trees (5.4 GB)
wrote ~3.6 GB of complete archives to the same root disk, because the payload
tarred every .git/objects pack the remote already has. Net gain was ~1.8 GB until
off-box upload + expiry (t_01a1d52db38e56aa).

What: when a standalone repo has remote-tracking refs of a remote with a network
URL (a local path is another tree that may itself be reclaimed), the complete
payload now leaves out
- every regular file under .git/objects/ (except info/alternates). In their place
  the payload carries objects/<n>.pack holding every *local* object the recorded
  tips do not reach: unpushed commits, staged-only blobs, stash, reflog-only and
  dangling objects. The set is local objects (loose + local .idx, alternates
  excluded) minus `rev-list --objects <tips>`; a partial clone's .promisor-pack
  objects count as promised (git's own rule) and its walk skips blobs
  (--filter=blob:none: 21 s -> 4 s on a blobless core clone). index-pack proves
  the pack holds exactly that set before use.
- clean tracked files: a regular, single-link file whose bytes hash to the blob
  that HEAD's nearest pushed commit(s) (rev-list --boundary HEAD ^tips) hold.
  The manifest keeps path, mode, size, mtime, sha256 and blob id.
Anything else (no network remote ref, shallow, sha256 repo, objects not a plain
dir, any plan error) keeps every object in the payload exactly as before.

Custody: the manifest becomes version 2 (omitted, omitted_repos with remotes,
recorded tip oids, pack sha256/size); verify_complete_archive checks those
records and pack hashes, and an older core refuses v2 instead of calling it
complete. The object files the plan was built from are re-checked against the
payload snapshot (a late object refuses the archive and keeps the tree), and an
omitted file rewritten after hashing is caught by the existing post-scan. Each
repo's meta.json says objects_omitted: "remote" with the restore steps; layout
v1 files (commits.bundle, tracked.diff, untracked.tar) are unchanged.
worker_archive_custody uploads every file of the archive dir, so the new pack
rides the same verified upload.

Restore: kanban_scratch_archive_omit.restore(archive, target, url_map=None)
extracts the tree (archived modes kept), clones each remote bare (partial
filter kept), fetches the recorded tips, moves those objects in, index-packs
the supplement, writes omitted files from their blobs (sha256-checked), then
checks every ref resolves and (non-partial) fsck --connectivity-only.

Bench (worker-4, daveey/hermes with one unpushed commit, one edit, one
untracked file):
- full clone: 1,348,263,296 -> 9,927,998 bytes (0.74 %), 10.7 s; restore from
  GitHub 135 s, `diff -r -x objects` identical, HEAD/status unchanged
- blobless + --reference clone: 281,901,596 -> 9,924,841 bytes (3.5 %), 6.4 s;
  restore 164 s, identical

Tests (tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_scratch_gc_archive.py): omitted archive
round-trips through _gc + restore (unpushed commit, staged blob, stash,
dangling object, modes, status); fixture under 10 % of source; local-path
remote keeps every object (v1); a late object or a rewritten omitted file keeps
the workspace; tampered pack/records refuse verify; a remote lacking the
objects fails restore loudly; blobless clone restores with lazy blobs; an
unpushed HEAD still omits files unchanged from its pushed parent. 336 tests
across the scratch/worktree-gc suites pass.
